A||N||25||Ditz88GT||Cold air intake?||||5||
Z||000000||Ditz88GT||07-11-2001||04:05 PM||||This may have come up before, but I have a few questions still.  I removed my air box and added a cone filter.  The O2 sensor was in the side of the air box, so I drilled a hole in the stock tube and inserted it in there.  This got me thinking...I wanted to get the filter out of the engine compartment (like Lisa and some others have done) and put it in the fender well behind the side scoops I have.  Well, the more I looked at it I realized that the metal hose going into the side of the stock tubing was hot air coming out of the valve cover.  Is there a way to avoid that?  Seems to defeat the purpose of cold air if it gets heated up right at the intake.  Also it looks like it's just an empty metal tube, shouldn't it have a PCV valve there or something?  I'm just wondering how/if others got around this.<P>Thanks<BR>~Ditz88GT<BR> <A HREF="http://go.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://go.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Neal||07-11-2001||04:07 PM||||That tube is just part of emmisions, feel free to pull the tube off and use a crankcase breather on the valve cover.  oh and plug the hole on the rubber tube||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||07-11-2001||04:10 PM||||I've got a CAI and a K&N breather filter on the valve cover.. and my cars been fine for quite a long time. the sensor you made a new port for is a MAT (Manifold Air Temp) sensor. not an O2.  you might wanna check with <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  for an intake tube||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||07-11-2001||04:25 PM||||Ah. The ol' hose from the valve cover pointed at the ground trick eh?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||07-11-2001||04:27 PM||||Don't even have to take it off. Get a pipe cutter. there little clamps with 2 round cutting wheels. Around $8 , cut the metal tube so it's straight , and doesn't curve into the rubber elbow<P>Then Get a rubber hose (around 1.5 ft should do it) clamp it to the metal valve cover tube , then mount a small K&N filter off of the rubber hose under the drivers side vent.<P>This is what I did on mine. I built Lisa's system , her's just goes into the elbow. I can't see this creating any real power loss. Cold air induction is more a means of getting more air into the motor.<P>I think only a setup Like 1FS2M6's would have a measurable difference in temperature over stock.  Lisa has the same system I have , I made that going on 5 years ago , along with another 10 or so I have insalled. Basically your getting the air from the same initial source , so temps won't differ to much<P>just a clarification<P>here's mine, you can see the chrome tube going into the fender , and the smaller K&N feeding the valve cover. I think with all the autocrossing at high RPm's there is probably some blow by at high RPMs. I know Grinthocks setup smoked a bity because the placement of his filter too close tot he valve cover , but I believe he has since fixed it<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/intake.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rob Durham||07-11-2001||11:11 PM||||Slammed Fiero<P>Got any more pics to show us of you cold air setup?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Max The Chainsaw||07-11-2001||11:36 PM||||Actually, the air inside that little tube doesn't go into the air intake. That tube supplies filtered air FROM the intake for the crankcase to replace the crankcase gasses pulled into the intake manifold by the PCV valve.<BR>In other words, the air goes away from the intake and not into it. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||07-12-2001||12:18 AM||||Thanks Max, thats what I thought.<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000008||Ditz88GT||07-12-2001||10:13 AM||||Ok, I think I'm starting to understand this a little better now, thanks for all your help.  But, now I have another question, what and where does the PCV valve hose connect to?  It seems to run under the intake manifold then I lose is.<P>~Ditz88GT<BR> <A HREF="http://go.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://go.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000009||88formula||07-12-2001||05:14 PM||||Question for all of you. Why would you spend your hard earned money on a cold air induction system for your car when it already has one? Some of these claimed cold air induction systems locate a cone filter inside the engine compartment were it sucks up some or all the HOT air from your engine compartment, not very good for performance I would say. The stock fiero system gets cold air from outside the car.<P>No flames just intelligent answers! <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Leadfoot||07-12-2001||05:23 PM||||Good point 88formula.  Someone here once measured the inside temperature of the engine compartment.  From what I recall the temps were toasty.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Slammed Fiero||07-12-2001||05:58 PM||||The answer is airflow<P>the stock air box may draw air in from the outside. Which is the same temp as the air drawn in by a cold air system. But the Cold air system allows for more air to flow. <P>Whats the diameter of the inlet on the factory air box , ID 2.5"?? Then it goes through the air box , which is restrictive to cut down on air intake noise (Induction noise). Most well thought out cold air systems have a fairly straight pipe going into the fender well , the air filter resides where the original air box used too , right behind the side vent.<P>A straight pipe has little or no turbulence as compared to the factory system where the air goes into the air box , into the air canister then the TB.<P>This also allows for greater overall air flow. were going from an inlet that is 2.5" in diameter to roughly 3.5"x6" for the filter. The oppertunity for air to be sucked into the  motor is far greater , and the air takes a much quicker path<P>hence the induction noise and sucking sound that is created.<P>Now I'm just waiting for someone to say 'Herb adams tried it and got no Hp" Well thats fine and dandy , I have a pic of his setup tested on one of his "Mules" The filter is colser to the engine than stock. HEnce hot air , hence NO Hp. And filter technology has come a long way since herb slung a wrench on a fiero.<P>Most people get 3-4 Rear wheel Hp , loss of 6 to 9 ft lbs of torque..<P>and thats why cold air systems work<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||07-12-2001||06:45 PM||||I don't know the validity of this website and the guys findings but read it anyway and let me know what you think Slam. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.org/text/firedup.txt</A>  <P>One more question; if you gain 3.4 rear wheel horsepower, which is allot, than how come you loose 6-9-foot pounds of torque? <P>My guess would be that with a much larger diameter intake pipe than stock you loose intake air velocity at low engine rpm, which means the engine draws in less air than a stock setup at low engine rpm. At higher rpm however air velocity is increased over stock and that may be why you gain more what I assume top end horsepower. It's a compromise then, much the same thing when choosing a set of cylinder heads and determining the intake runner size. <P>I bet if you gain a few top end horsepower than even though you may loose 6-9-foot pounds of peak torque you may gain more top end torque over stock. If I had a dyno sheet, table, or graph I could test what the effects would be in my Drag program for vehicle performance. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||07-12-2001||07:04 PM||||Everything that you can do outside of internal modifications to how the engine works will result in a compromise. My feeling is such that I value torque more than HP at the moment. I'm not a great believer in "Cold Air Induction" systems, as they tend not to do too much in my favor. HOWEVER, I will be doing it just for the hoover noise  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000014||Slammed Fiero||07-12-2001||09:53 PM||||My soul reason for the cold air is autocrossing and track events. The Fieor has enough torque for autocrossing , but falls on it's face in upper Rpm's. Both tracks I run have only one low rpm corner where you need the torque of the motor to pull past other cars. I'm usually classed with modded Crx's and such. Off the hair pin and 90 degree turn where speeds fall below 40mph I still have enough torque to pull past them right off the corner. Keep in mind I also have no cat , increase in HP , loss of TQ again.<P>The fiero has a good bottom end grunt to push it's lofty curb weight around. Combine that with the 3.65 4spd and you have a great tranny for the track. I can basically shift between 2nd and 3rd with one upshift to 4th for the long 100mph+ straight.<P>Anything to get rid of the fiero's hump in the torque curve is a good comprimise for me...but not everyone is into track racing , lotsa drag racers out there too..<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||07-12-2001||10:15 PM||||I ran the stock airbox with my TDC swap using 3" tubing and only noticed a small difference(OK,big noise difference)when I later switched over to a larger Buick rubber intake hose(with air temp sensor hole)and put the air cleaner element out into the fender with no canister to shroud it.<BR>Can't imagine it'd make much difference on anything with stock heads/TB.<BR>I liked the way the stock intake tube gradually tapered down a size from the canister to the TB;cool design.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ditz88GT||07-13-2001||10:14 AM||||So does anybody know where the hose on the PCV connect to?<P>~Ditz88GT<BR> <A HREF="http://go.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://go.to/fiero</A> <P>(Edited for spelling error)<p>[This message has been edited by Ditz88GT (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000017||Leadfoot||07-13-2001||10:41 AM||||According to the website given by 88formula<BR>removing the stock airbox gives no noticeable performance gains.<P>Here is a small quote: <P>"In searching for more power, my race crew tested the Fiero V6 engine on the <BR>dyno without the stock air cleaner and inlet system, and we were surprised <BR>to find that this produced no change in power. Apparently, the Pontiac <BR>engineers did a good job on these parts because they work well, even though <BR>they look restrictive."<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||1FST2M6||07-13-2001||03:16 PM||||their testing neglected to introduce air pressure or wind on the body of the car... the whole theory behind the NACA duct is air pressure and it's constant search for relief. the intake tube that chris west makes booth the long tube and resonator replacement will add hp while the car is at speed above 35mph... having constructed and dyno tested similar units on my cars. <P>just my findings and nickles worth...<BR> <P><BR>ditz..  PVC? Poly-vinyl-chloride, or PCV Positive case vent (truncated)  it connects back to the intake a negative pressure area to aid in the PCVs function<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||terryk||07-13-2001||03:48 PM||||Using the mass on my 3800SC, I've found that the stock Fiero intake tract is good for about 180 gm/s of air. Neither the 2.8L nor 3.1L can hit that. 150-160 maybe.<P>When I use just a cone filter on the throttle body I can get 210 gm/s of air. Of course it heat soaks in mere seconds....<P>On a friend's DOHC conversion we could see the engine actually pull a vaccum in the stock air canister. That's around 180 gm/s of air. There would be a dip in the manifold pressure as the engine crossed 6800 RPM and up to 8000.<P>I agree that for most 660 engines, the Fiero intake tract is good and not the problem. When you get to the 3.4L DOHC, I'd consider some changes.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||b lo 0||07-13-2001||04:01 PM||||IMHO, the best for performance would be to replace the stock airbox with an aluminum cube with enough room for a velocity stack and can filter, replace the hose with mandrel bent tubing, wrap the whole thing in heat reflective tape, and use a Holley scoop on the outside.  I think it's economical, and should build between 5 and 15 HP (Travis, any ideas?).  I will be putting something like this on my car, I'll let you know how it goes<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">      <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000021||qwikgta||07-13-2001||10:17 PM||||This is what I did for my duke.  It is louder but I didnt notice any diff in perf until I added the header and open exhaust.  I feel a pull on the 2-3 shift and 3-4 shift.  It feels great.  It is too loud and I will be getting a supertrapp on it when I get it in the mail, hopefully tomorrow.<P>Rob<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_engine_small.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1july2_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1july5.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
